WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.241
Picture a work group meeting. A self-advocate is typing a

00:00:03.241 --> 00:00:06.481
reply on a speech device. It takes time. The discussion

00:00:06.481 --> 00:00:09.722
moves on. A parent offers a guess about what the

00:00:09.722 --> 00:00:12.963
self-advocate means. A case manager answers the parent. In under

00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:16.203
a minute, the person most affected has been talked past.

00:00:16.203 --> 00:00:19.120
Nobody intended it. Everyone was trying to be helpful.

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:23.024
This lesson is about that minute. For eligibility, a disability

00:00:23.024 --> 00:00:26.579
can be a set of criteria. For many people, it

00:00:26.579 --> 00:00:30.133
is also an identity and a culture. Some Deaf people

00:00:30.133 --> 00:00:33.687
belong to a community built around American Sign Language. A

00:00:33.687 --> 00:00:37.241
study of 267 deaf adults found three identities in roughly

00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:40.796
equal shares: culturally Deaf, culturally hearing and bicultural. So there

00:00:40.796 --> 00:00:44.350
is no single Deaf experience, and no single right accommodation.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.740
You have to ask.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.848
Identity matters for well-being too. In a survey of 226

00:00:49.848 --> 00:00:53.606
adults with mobility disabilities, a stronger disability identity went with

00:00:53.606 --> 00:00:57.364
higher life satisfaction. That study shows a link, not a

00:00:57.364 --> 00:01:01.123
cause. It still points away from treating disability only as

00:01:01.123 --> 00:01:02.250
something to fix.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.161
Now consider your own assumptions. In interviews with 153 people

00:01:06.161 --> 00:01:09.723
with disabilities, more than half of those with serious disabilities

00:01:09.723 --> 00:01:13.284
rated their quality of life as good or excellent. In

00:01:13.284 --> 00:01:16.845
a national survey, 82 percent of physicians assumed such lives

00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:20.406
were worse. When professionals expect less, they turn to family

00:01:20.406 --> 00:01:23.968
members, rush past slow answers and choose safety over a

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:24.680
person's goals.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.575
Minnesota law sets a different expectation. For disability waiver services,

00:01:28.575 --> 00:01:32.121
it presumes people can make informed choices. It requires information

00:01:32.121 --> 00:01:35.666
in an accessible format and ample time to decide. A

00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:39.212
meeting run at the speed of the fastest talker does

00:01:39.212 --> 00:01:40.630
not meet that standard.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.886
So what do you do in that minute? Pause the

00:01:43.886 --> 00:01:46.792
group. Say you want to hear the self-advocate directly. Wait

00:01:46.792 --> 00:01:49.698
for the typing to finish. Then ask the parent what

00:01:49.698 --> 00:01:52.605
they want to add for themselves. You have not scolded

00:01:52.605 --> 00:01:55.220
anyone. You have shown the room whose voice decides.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.193
Before the next meeting, change the design. Ask every member

00:01:59.193 --> 00:02:02.816
about access and preferred terms. Send materials early. Cut the

00:02:02.816 --> 00:02:06.439
agenda. Use rounds, and wait through silence. Afterward, send the

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:10.061
summary to members so they can correct it. Person-centered planning

00:02:10.061 --> 00:02:13.684
shows real but moderate benefits, on a weak research base.

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:17.307
The benefits seem to depend on whether the person actually

00:02:17.307 --> 00:02:20.930
directs what happens. Your meeting design is where that starts.
